# XTMODKEYS and XTQMODKEYS for modifyOtherKeys (resource 4): xterm's ctlseqs. What the level does to the
# keys a host sends is the host's to encode (`wormhole_vt::input`, tested beside it); the terminal keeps
# the level and answers the query.

test modifyOtherKeys starts at 0 and the query says so
spec xterm ctlseqs XTQMODKEYS: CSI ? Pp m is answered with the XTMODKEYS sequence that sets the value, CSI > Pp ; Pv m
size 10 4
feed \e[?4m
reply \e[>4;0m

test CSI > 4 ; Pv m sets the level
spec xterm ctlseqs XTMODKEYS: CSI > Pp ; Pv m sets the resource Pp (4 is modifyOtherKeys) to Pv; xterm ctlseqs Alt and Meta Keys: levels 1 and 2
size 10 4
feed \e[>4;1m\e[?4m
reply \e[>4;1m
feed \e[>4;2m\e[?4m
reply \e[>4;2m
feed \e[>4;0m\e[?4m
reply \e[>4;0m

test the second parameter left out resets the resource
spec xterm ctlseqs XTMODKEYS: if the second parameter is omitted, the resource is reset to its initial value
size 10 4
feed \e[>4;2m\e[>4m\e[?4m
reply \e[>4;0m

test no parameters at all reset every resource
spec xterm ctlseqs XTMODKEYS: if no parameters are given, all resources are reset to their initial values
size 10 4
feed \e[>4;2m\e[>m\e[?4m
reply \e[>4;0m

test a soft reset puts the level back
spec xterm ctlseqs DECSTR: reset key-format and key-modifier modes to the values set by resources (modifyOtherKeys among them)
size 10 4
feed \e[>4;2m\e[!p\e[?4m
reply \e[>4;0m

test a full reset puts the level back
spec xterm ctlseqs RIS: full reset
size 10 4
feed \e[>4;1m\ec\e[?4m
reply \e[>4;0m
